  "bugVerdicts": [
    {
      "description": "retry() declared as plain (non-suspend) fun but calls delay(1000) and invokes a suspend lambda block(); does not compile.",
      "verdict": "CONFIRMED",
      "reasoning": "Verified android-patterns/SKILL.md lines 156-165. `fun <T> retry(times: Int, block: suspend () -> T): T` is NOT marked `suspend`, yet its body calls `block()` (typed `suspend () -> T`) and `delay(1000)` (a suspend function). Both can only be invoked from a coroutine or another suspend function, so the compiler rejects this ('Suspend function should be called only from a coroutine or another suspend function'). The `repeat { }` lambda is inline so its body runs in retry's own non-suspend context — no rescue. Signature must be `suspend fun retry(...)`. It is presented under the recommended 'Higher-Order Functions' section. Genuine, unambiguous compile error. Critical severity is fair."
    },
    {
      "description": "retry() catches Exception, swallowing CancellationException and breaking structured concurrency; the loop keeps retrying/delaying after its scope is cancelled.",
      "verdict": "PARTIAL",
      "reasoning": "The factual half is true: android-patterns lines 160-163 `catch (e: Exception) { delay(1000) }` does catch CancellationException (on JVM it extends Exception), and the idiomatic fix is a preceding `catch (e: CancellationException) { throw e }` — which the sibling rcosteira skill literally cites at line 140 ('// rethrow first — a broad catch would swallow CancellationException'). But the described FAILURE SCENARIO is inaccurate for this specific code: after the catch, `delay(1000)` executes, and in a cancelled coroutine `delay` is itself a cancellation point that immediately re-throws CancellationException (it does NOT wait 1000ms and does NOT loop). So genuine job-cancellation propagates out of retry via the delay rather than being swallowed into an endless retry loop. The residual real defect is only the missing CancellationException rethrow (a spurious, still-active-scope CancellationException from e.g. a nested withTimeout would be treated as retryable). Real anti-pattern, but the stated consequence ('keeps retrying and delaying after cancellation') does not occur. Also moot in isolation since bug #1 means it never compiles."
    },
    {
      "description": "fetchUser() wraps a suspend network call in runCatching, catching CancellationException and converting a cancelled call to Result.failure so cancellation never propagates.",
      "verdict": "CONFIRMED",
      "reasoning": "Verified android-patterns lines 140-142: `suspend fun fetchUser(id: String): Result<User> = runCatching { api.getUser(id).toDomain() }`, marked '✅' at line 139. `runCatching` catches Throwable, including CancellationException. `api.getUser(id)` is a cancellable suspend call; if the coroutine is cancelled during it, the CancellationException is captured into `Result.failure` and the function returns NORMALLY — unlike the retry case there is no subsequent cancellation point (no delay) to re-throw, so cancellation is genuinely swallowed. The caller chain (lines 145-150: `.onFailure { showError(it) }`) would then render an error instead of respecting cancellation, breaking structured concurrency. Well-documented Kotlin anti-pattern; correct form is try/catch that rethrows CancellationException. Major severity fair."
    },
    {
      "description": "Side-effects Channel collected in plain LaunchedEffect(Unit) with no repeatOnLifecycle, so effects (navigation, snackbar) fire while the screen is backgrounded.",
      "verdict": "CONFIRMED",
      "reasoning": "Verified mvi-architecture lines 119-128. The collector `LaunchedEffect(Unit) { viewModel.sideEffects.collect { ... NavigateToDetail/ShowSnackbar/NavigateToLogin ... } }` has no lifecycle gating. The composition stays alive while the Activity is STOPPED, so an effect emitted while backgrounded (e.g. an async result completing after the user backgrounds the app) is delivered immediately: navigation stacks off-screen / a snackbar shows to nobody. The buffered Channel only behaves correctly if collection pauses when backgrounded, which is exactly what rcosteira mandates via repeatOnLifecycle (lines 90-99) and what its buffer-and-replay rationale (line 57) requires. Real, recognized lifecycle defect. It is a subtle correctness/best-practice issue rather than a guaranteed crash, so 'major' is on the strong side but defensible."
    },
